In article <2304@wheaton.UUCP> water@wheaton.UUCP (Paul Waterman) writes:
>The first part was well and good - gives everyone some clues as
>to how a successful game of nethack can be played, some strategy tips, etc.

>Everything from the screen shot of the last level on, however, was pure
>self-glorification, and a TOTAL waste of net bandwidth.

Actually I would have thought that one might have considered the first
part the "self-glorification", in that I'm describing my game, as
opposed to simply listing my necessary inventory items and intrinsics
afterwards.

I did a screen shot of the Endlevel, to show what my stats,
experience, number of moves required, score before offering the
Amulet, etc was at the end of the game, yielding significant
information. Actually, I think magic mapped snapshots of the
Stronghold and Endlevel should be included in the spoiler files. The
first time I encountered them I was baffled as to what they were, what
was on the rest of the level, and how I was to pass them; i notice
that many of the posted questions on the net deal with finding that
elusive secret passage on the Endlevel, how to get past the
drawbridge, where to find the wand of wishing, etc.

Checking my final inventory will show that at the end I only have the
real essentials left. A person may want to know what the better
weapons, armor, and the more useful rings, wands, and tools to have in
order to complete the game are. Of course a good intrinsic list is
vital to successfully winning, and I've found that certain spellbooks
are almost worth wishing for, such as charm monster, which I once
found *very* useful when I was stuck in a hallway in the castle
between a Troll and a Rust Monster, or magic mapping when on your way
up from the wizard if you haven't already been on the above levels...

